<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gahadavala dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1080 CE – 1194 CE (approx.) · capital Kannauj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North India's last great Hindu kingdom on the Ganges before the Ghurid conquest, ruling Kannauj and the holy city of Varanasi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Gahadavalas rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley in the late 11th century, filling the vacuum left by the collapse of Pratihara and Kalachuri power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandradeva">Chandradeva</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> around 1090 and also controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a>, founding a line that soon dominated the middle Ganges plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings issued hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate land grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, the main source for the dynasty, and styled themselves protectors of the sacred sites of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kashi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govindachandra-of-the-gahadavala-dynasty">Govindachandra</a>, the most powerful ruler, fought the Palas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid</a> raiders and patronised learning; his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaradevi">Kumaradevi</a> endowed a Buddhist monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>. Revenue came from the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a>, and a special levy, the Turushkadanda, may have funded defence against Turkic incursions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a> was defeated and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chandawar">Battle of Chandawar</a> in 1194, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurids</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores</a>, claimed descent from the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gahadavala-dynasty.jpg" alt="Gahadavala dynasty" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: User:Ancientcoinsofindia1, English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govindchandra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1080 CE – 1194 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kannauj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chandradeva, Govindachandra, Jayachandra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gahadavalas of Kannauj, Rathores of Kannauj</dd></dl></aside></div>

